# Challenge130 — bento/ubuntu-22.04 TEST COPY (not the working baseline)
#
# Swapped generic/ubuntu2204 -> bento/ubuntu-22.04, same version PROVISIONING
# already runs successfully (202401.31.0). Everything else is unchanged from
# the working Vagrantfile in this same directory.
#
# Why this box, why now: PROVISIONING (bento/ubuntu-22.04, single adapter,
# no r2r links, no FRR/nmcli role usage) confirmed:
#   - net.ifnames=0 biosdevname=0 present -> eth0/eth1 legacy naming holds
#   - Vagrant's private_network write (50-vagrant.yaml, renderer: networkd,
#     eth1: addresses: [...]) is byte-shape-identical to what generic/
#     ubuntu2204's router-1 produces -> looks like Vagrant's own behavior,
#     not a generic-box-specific default
#   - cloud-init has network: {config: disabled} -> won't clobber netplan
#     on later boots
# None of that exercises the one thing this scenario actually tests: r2r
# links + FRR + the nmcli-managed/unmanaged branching in network/interface,
# network/gateway, network/r2r_link under multiple adapters, and whether
# router-1's r2r degree 4 (6 total adapters) trips the same PCI-bridge
# overflow signature fix_router_vnets.py/fix_router_netplan.py were built
# against on generic/ubuntu2204 — or a different one, or none at all.
#
# ORIGINAL KNOWN RISK (see GOAD_Scenario_Build_Summary.md, Challenge101
# session log), carried over unchanged since it's about VMware Workstation
# adapter counts, not the guest OS:
# router-1 is the hub (r2r degree 4) and therefore needs 6 total adapters
# (NAT + management + 4 r2r links). On VMware Workstation this is the exact
# adapter count that previously triggered a PCI-bridge slot overflow, causing
# router-1's guest interface naming to desync from VMX slot order and 5 of
# its adapters to bind to the wrong vmnets. If router-1 comes up but is
# unreachable via plain `ssh` (while `vagrant ssh router-1` still works),
# see that session's "Major finding" section for the MAC-address-based
# GUI-bypass fix — do not rely on vagrant reload/halt/up to fix it, those
# will re-assert the wrong mapping every time. Whether the same fix applies
# as-is on bento, or needs its detection logic adjusted, is exactly what
# this test copy exists to find out.

